electromagnetic-induction-turns-options-3 = 400
electromagnetic-induction-build-question = We join a coil of { $turns } turns to a milliammeter with its zero in the middle: the needle swings the way the current goes. We put a bar magnet in the coil; then pull it out fast and push it in fast, north pole first, then slowly, and then push it in fast south pole first. At last we keep the magnet still and slip the coil onto it fast. Each time we note which way and how far the needle swung. When does a current flow in the coil?
electromagnetic-induction-answer-why = A current appears in the coil only while the magnetic flux through its turns changes — that is, while the magnet and the coil move against each other. A still magnet, even inside, makes no current. The faster the flux changes, the larger the induced EMF — and the current. And the current flows so that its own field hinders the change of the flux (Lenz’s law): the magnet coming in is pushed back by the coil, going out — pulled. So in or out, north or south pole first — the current goes opposite ways. And it does not matter which moves, the magnet or the coil: only their motion against each other counts.
electromagnetic-induction-answer-say = The current flows only while the magnet moves against the coil!
electromagnetic-induction-answer-say-2 = Let us look at the milliammeter.
electromagnetic-induction-answer-text = A coil of { $turns } turns; the circuit’s resistance (the coil and the meter) ≈ { $Rc } Ω. The magnet still in the coil — { $value }. Pulled out fast — { $value2 }; pushed in north pole first fast — { $value3 }, slowly — { $value4 }; south pole first fast — { $value5 }; the coil slipped onto the still magnet — { $value6 }. The EMF ε = I·R: fast ≈ { $value7 } V, slowly ≈ { $value8 } V — the faster, the larger. The current changes sign when the magnet is pulled out instead of pushed in and when it is turned the other pole first.
